<div class="mx-auto max-w-md">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Settings</h1>

  <%# What the last thing you did here actually did. #472 needs it most: connecting to
      Withings leaves the site, comes back through a redirect, and can fail in four
      distinguishable ways -- so a page that looked identical afterwards would be the app
      refusing to say whether it worked.

      role="status" rather than alert: it is the outcome of something just asked for, not an
      interruption. Read once and gone, so a reload does not re-announce it.

      Above the section index and outside every section, because it is the answer to the last
      thing asked rather than a fact about one setting -- and because the flows that set it are
      the ones that come back to the top of the page. #529 left it here for that reason: a
      notice inside the Wearables block would be jumped past by the very redirect that wrote
      it. %>
  <%- if @notice %>
    <p role="status" class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-sky-800 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <%= @notice %>
    </p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# Two forms, not one -- four of them now. A week start and a plate inventory are unrelated
      answers, and a single Save across both would mean changing either required re-submitting
      the other. They share a page because they are the same kind of thing -- a per-account
      preference about how the app should behave -- and splitting them put settings in two
      places (#189).

      No units here. That was the largest part of #189 and it was dropped outright: pounds
      everywhere, no kg, so there is nothing to choose. %>

  <%# A way to reach one of these without scrolling past the other four. #529.

      The issue asks for submenus under the settings dropdown, and there is no dropdown: the
      top bar is six flat links and `settings` is one of them. Asked what was wanted, the
      answer was the page rather than the bar -- group what is already here under the five
      names below, in this order, and leave the nav exactly as it is. So nothing above this
      line moves, `assistants` keeps its place in the bar, and a spec pins both.

      Anchors rather than pages or disclosures, and what each would have cost is the argument.

      A page per section -- /settings/plates, /settings/wearables -- is the shape the word
      "submenu" suggests and the one that breaks what is here: four of these five sections are
      forms that post independently, one of them posts two racks in a single submission on
      purpose, and dealing them out across routes means four more routes, four more views and
      four more redirects to get wrong, on a page whose only actual problem is its length. It
      also changes what a save means, which is the one thing this could not do.

      Collapsing them behind taps hides four in five by default, which is worse on the phone
      this is for: a lifter who came to change one plate count taps twice instead of once, and
      the open/shut state then has to survive a save and a reload or the page is lying about
      where it was. It would also be a fourth copy of the disclosure chevron, which
      named_styles_spec pins at three.

      So the page stays one page, every form stays where it was and posts exactly what it
      posted, and the fragment does the work. It renders for nothing, it survives a reload
      because it is in the URL, the back button undoes it, it needs no JavaScript, and the
      saves land back on the section they were made in rather than at the top. What it buys is
      smaller than a menu looks -- five links, not a tree -- and it is the whole of what was
      asked for: a way to get to one of these directly.

      The links are tap targets rather than a sentence with five words underlined in it,
      because this row is the thing being aimed at with a thumb before anything else on the
      page is. %>
  <nav aria-label="Settings sections" class="mt-4 border-y border-gray-200">
    <ul class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5">
      <%- { 'weight-plates' => 'Weight plates', 'wearables' => 'Wearables', 'ai-agents' => 'AI agents',
            'time-and-week' => 'Time and week', 'session-length' => 'Session length' }.each do |anchor, name| %>
        <li>
          <a href="#<%= anchor %>"
             class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center text-sm font-semibold text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">
            <%= name %>
          </a>
        </li>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <%# The rack first, because it is the longest section and the one most often come back to:
      plates get sold and bought, and every number the app prints is rounded to what this says.
      #529 asked for this order by name.

      "Weight plates" rather than "Equipment", which is also theirs. Equipment was a heading
      that could have held the bar, the rack, a belt and a watch, and this section holds
      exactly two racks' worth of plates and the two handles they go on.

      scroll-mt keeps the heading off the very top edge when the fragment lands on it, so a
      section arrives looking like the top of something rather than like a line that happens to
      be first. %>
  <section id="weight-plates" class="scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Weight plates</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      What you lift with. Every weight the app works out &mdash; warmups, working sets, the
      plate breakdown, and how much a programme adds after a good week &mdash; is rounded to
      something this rack can actually load.
    </p>

    <%# What changing the rack did to the sessions already written. #439.
        Saving used to affect only the next generation, so a block already on the calendar kept
        whatever the app had believed about the plates on the day it was generated -- which is
        how the same movement came to be prescribed at 44 one week and 45 the next, 45 being a
        dumbbell a 4 lb handle cannot build. Now it reaches them, and this says so: a lifter who
        opens next Monday and finds a different number on it should have been told here rather
        than left to notice.

        Only when something actually moved. The count is of sessions whose weights changed, not
        of sessions rewritten, so correcting a typo in a plate count does not announce that it
        rewrote a fortnight of training when it changed nothing at all.

        Inside this section rather than beside the notice at the top, and it is the save's own
        redirect that makes the difference: the rack form comes back to #weight-plates, so an
        announcement about the rack has to be in the part of the page that lands under the
        thumb. %>
    <%- if @rerounded.positive? %>
      <p class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-sky-800 bg-sky-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-gray-700">
        Your rack changed, so
        <strong><%= @rerounded %> upcoming <%= @rerounded == 1 ? 'session was' : 'sessions were' %></strong>
        rewritten onto weights it can load. Sets you had already marked as done were left exactly
        as they were.
      </p>
    <%- end %>

    <form method="post" action="/settings" class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      <%== csrf_tag('/settings') %>

      <%# Two racks, named. #369 asks for the plate inventory to be "separated into a barbell
          plates and a dumbbell pairs/plates option", and the separation is what makes either of
          them readable: without a heading the second block of ten spin buttons is ten more of
          the first, and the counts mean different things. %>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Barbell</h3>

      <div>
        <%# Named rather than just "Bar", which is #439's complaint about every field on this
            form: the label has to say which number it wants, because the wrong reading is not
            obviously wrong anywhere afterwards. %>
        <label for="bar_weight" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">
          The empty bar, on its own
        </label>
        <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2">
          <input type="number" id="bar_weight" name="bar_weight" min="1" step="any" autocomplete="off"
                 value="<%= weight_label(@equipment.bar_weight) %>"
                 class="<%= field_style %> w-28 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
          <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">lb</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <fieldset>
        <legend class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Plates</legend>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
          Pairs, not single plates &mdash; a bar is loaded on both sides. Set a count to zero
          to say you no longer own it. Your smallest plate decides the smallest jump you can
          make: <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= @equipment.increment %> lb</span>.
        </p>
        <%# What the answers add up to, which is the check #439 asks for. Derived through the
            same enumeration the generator prescribes from, so a lifter who read "pairs" as
            "plates" sees a top end twice what their rack can really do -- which restating the
            numbers they typed would never have shown them. %>
        <%- if (range = loadable_range(@equipment.loadable_totals)) %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
            That loads <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= range %></span>.
          </p>
        <%- end %>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          <%- Tectonic::Equipment::OFFERED.each do |denomination| %>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <%# A real label, not a lookalike span: the "45 lb" text was beside each box
                  with nothing connecting the two, so a screen reader heard ten anonymous
                  spin buttons -- on the form that decides what the whole app can load.
                  The id swaps the dot out of 2.5 and 1.25 because a dot in an id is legal
                  HTML that CSS and querySelector both read as a class boundary. %>
              <label for="plates_<%= denomination.to_s.tr('.', '_') %>"
                     class="w-16 text-right font-mono text-sm text-gray-900">
                <%= denomination %> lb
              </label>
              <input type="number" min="0" step="1" autocomplete="off"
                     id="plates_<%= denomination.to_s.tr('.', '_') %>"
                     name="plates[<%= denomination %>]"
                     value="<%= @equipment.pairs.fetch(denomination, 0) %>"
                     class="<%= field_style %> w-20 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
              <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">pairs</span>
            </div>
          <%- end %>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <%# The second rack. #369.
          Until now the app knew what plates an account owns and nothing at all about its
          dumbbells, so it assumed fives -- which #259 named as an assumption when it made it.
          That is right for a fixed rack and wrong in both directions for an adjustable set: a
          pair of Powerblocks steps in 2.5 at the bottom, so 27.5 is loadable and was being
          rounded away; and a handle with only 5s and 10s on the shelf cannot make 22.5, which
          the assumption cheerfully prescribed.

          Blank is the ordinary answer and says so, because most people have a fixed rack and
          nobody should have to fill this in to be described correctly. It is also the way back:
          clearing the handle returns the account to the assumption, which would otherwise be
          unsayable once it had been said. %>
      <h3 class="border-t border-gray-200 pt-6 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
        Dumbbells
      </h3>

      <div>
        <%# "Handle" was the worst of them, and #439 says so: it is ambiguous between one handle
            and the pair, and the two readings differ by a factor of two. %>
        <label for="dumbbell_handle_weight" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">
          One handle, with nothing on it
        </label>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
          Only for plate-loaded or adjustable dumbbells. Leave it blank for a fixed rack, which
          the app treats as running in
          <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= Tectonic::Equipment::DUMBBELL_INCREMENT %> lb</span> steps.
        </p>
        <%- if @equipment.dumbbell_handle_weight&.positive? %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
            <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= weight_label(@equipment.dumbbell_handle_weight) %> lb</span>
            each, <%= weight_label(@equipment.dumbbell_handle_weight * 2) %> lb for the pair.
          </p>
        <%- end %>
        <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2">
          <input type="number" id="dumbbell_handle_weight" name="dumbbell_handle_weight" min="0" step="any"
                 autocomplete="off" value="<%= weight_label(@equipment.dumbbell_handle_weight) %>"
                 class="<%= field_style %> w-28 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
          <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">lb</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <fieldset>
        <legend class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Dumbbell plates</legend>
        <%# "Per dumbbell" is the whole of what has to be said here, and it has to be said: an
            adjustable set is sold as two handles sharing one plate set, so a lifter counting the
            shelf is counting what goes on one dumbbell. Asking across both would make every
            number on this form twice what anybody would write down. %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
          Pairs per dumbbell &mdash; one pair is one plate on each end. Your smallest decides the
          smallest jump:
          <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= @equipment.dumbbell_increment %> lb</span>.
        </p>
        <%# Said twice, because since #439 a movement says how many dumbbells it is done with and
            the answers are different. Every plate size has to go on both ends of every handle in
            use, so a matched pair spends two pairs of a size where a single spends one and
            reaches roughly half as far up the shelf.

            This is also the shortest explanation of a thing that would otherwise look like a
            bug: a dumbbell bench prescribed lighter than a single-arm row off the same plates. %>
        <%- if (ranges = dumbbell_ranges(@equipment)) %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
            One dumbbell loads <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= ranges[:one] %></span>.
            A matched pair loads <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= ranges[:pair] %></span> each,
            because both handles need the same plates.
          </p>
        <%- end %>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          <%- Tectonic::Equipment::OFFERED.each do |denomination| %>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <label for="dumbbell_plates_<%= denomination.to_s.tr('.', '_') %>"
                     class="w-16 text-right font-mono text-sm text-gray-900">
                <%= denomination %> lb
              </label>
              <input type="number" min="0" step="1" autocomplete="off"
                     id="dumbbell_plates_<%= denomination.to_s.tr('.', '_') %>"
                     name="dumbbell_plates[<%= denomination %>]"
                     value="<%= @equipment.dumbbell_pairs.fetch(denomination, 0) %>"
                     class="<%= field_style %> w-20 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
              <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">pairs</span>
            </div>
          <%- end %>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <button type="submit"
              class="<%= button_style %> flex w-full justify-center bg-lime-500 px-3 py-1.5
                     text-sm font-semibold leading-6 text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
    </form>
  </section>

  <%# Reading your own bodyweight from Withings. #472.

      Its own form, outside the equipment one above, on the same argument the page already
      makes twice: these are unrelated answers and one Save across them would mean changing
      the rack to connect a scale.

      Three states rather than two. A grant revoked from Withings' own app leaves a row here
      that no longer buys anything, and a page saying "connected" about it would be wrong in
      the one way that is hard to notice -- readings simply stop arriving.

      "Wearables" rather than "Withings", which is #529's renaming and is the one with an
      argument behind it: Withings is one wearable of several this app might read one day --
      Apple Health is a deferred ticket -- and a section named after a vendor has to be renamed
      the day a second one arrives, taking every link and every spec that mentions it along.
      The brand keeps the heading below, where it belongs: it is which wearable, not what the
      section is for, and a second one lands here as another h3 rather than as a rewrite. %>
  <section id="wearables" class="scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Wearables</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      What the app may read from something you wear. One device so far, and everything it
      reads sits alongside your training rather than replacing any of it.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Withings</h3>

    <%# The connection is asked about *before* the credentials, and the order is load-bearing.
        Disconnecting is a local delete -- it forgets a row, it does not call Withings -- so it
        must stay available even on a deployment whose credentials have been removed. The other
        way round, a lifter who was connected when the keys went away could no longer revoke
        what they had granted, which is the one thing they must always be able to do. %>
    <%- if @withings[:state] == :absent && !@withings_configured %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
        This deployment has no Withings credentials set, so there is nothing to connect to.
        That is the ordinary state of a local checkout.
      </p>
    <%- elsif @withings[:state] == :absent %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
        Connect your Withings account and the app can read your weight and body composition
        alongside your training. It reads; it never writes anything back.
      </p>
      <form method="post" action="/settings/withings/connect" class="mt-3">
        <%== csrf_tag('/settings/withings/connect') %>
        <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                      text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Connect Withings</button>
      </form>
    <%- else %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
        <%- if @withings[:state] == :stale %>
          Connected on <%= @withings[:connected_at].strftime('%-d %b %Y') %>, but the permission
          has expired and could not be renewed &mdash; usually because it was revoked from
          Withings itself. Connect again to resume.
        <%- else %>
          Connected on <%= @withings[:connected_at].strftime('%-d %b %Y') %>.
          <%- if @withings[:synced_at] %>
            Last read <%= @withings[:synced_at].strftime('%-d %b at %H:%M') %>.
          <%- else %>
            Nothing has been read yet.
          <%- end %>
        <%- end %>
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <%- if @withings[:state] == :stale && @withings_configured %>
          <form method="post" action="/settings/withings/connect">
            <%== csrf_tag('/settings/withings/connect') %>
            <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                          text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Reconnect</button>
          </form>
        <%- end %>
        <form method="post" action="/settings/withings/disconnect">
          <%== csrf_tag('/settings/withings/disconnect') %>
          <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border border-gray-300 px-3 text-sm
                                        font-semibold text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">Disconnect</button>
        </form>
      </div>
      <%# Said plainly because it is the question anybody hesitating over Disconnect has. %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500">
        Disconnecting stops the app asking for more. Measurements already read stay where they are.
      </p>
      <%# The review list's permanent address. #534.

          /workouts/withings had nothing at all pointing at it: a lifter who ran the backfill,
          closed the terminal and came back on Sunday could only reach their own questions by
          remembering a URL. The prompt that actually catches somebody is on /workouts, and it
          is only there while something is waiting, which is right for a prompt and wrong for an
          address -- a screen that can only be found when it has work on it cannot be gone back
          to, and a lifter who wants to check whether they finished has nowhere to look.

          So the link that is always here is here, with the connection that produces the
          questions. It is the one place in the app where a lifter already reasons about the
          watch, nothing else in the app moves when it appears, and it is asked about the same
          way it is found -- which is what the nav could not offer at any price: a permanent nav
          entry is carried on every page by every lifter, and a conditional one moves the entries
          after it.

          It says the count rather than only linking, so the link can be decided about without
          being followed. Nought is worth printing here and is not worth printing on /workouts:
          there, the absence of the line is the message, and a box saying nothing is waiting
          would be clutter on a page about something else. Here the link is unconditional, so
          the sentence has to be able to say the screen is empty. %>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
        <a href="/workouts/withings" class="font-semibold text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Matches from
          your watch</a>
        &mdash;
        <%- if @withings_waiting.positive? %>
          <%= @withings_waiting %> session<%= 's' unless @withings_waiting == 1 %> from your history
          <%= @withings_waiting == 1 ? 'is' : 'are' %> waiting for an answer.
        <%- else %>
          nothing is waiting for an answer.
        <%- end %>
      </p>
    <%- end %>
  </section>

  <%# The connector, named here as a section and left living where it already lives. #529.

      It is the odd one of the five. The other four are settings in the strict sense -- answers
      stored against this account, changed by a form and saved -- and this one is a walk: an
      address to paste into an assistant, the instructions for where each client hides the
      paste box, and the list of what is currently connected with a Disconnect beside each.

      So two things were deliberately not done. The whole of /connections was not dragged in
      here, because that walk leaves the site and comes back through an approval screen and
      wants a page of its own rather than a fifth of one -- and because a settings page that
      held it would be asking a lifter to scroll past a credential-shaped URL to reach their
      plate counts. And the markup was not copied, because two copies of that URL is two places
      to change it and one of them will be missed; a section that links is a section that
      cannot drift.

      `assistants` also keeps its place in the top bar pointing at the same page, which was
      asked for in as many words. It is #534's argument about a nav entry, run backwards: people
      learn where things are by position, and taking an entry away moves every entry after it
      just as surely as adding one does.

      The count is here for the same reason the matches count is in the section above -- it is
      what lets somebody decide not to follow the link -- and it is one query that reads no
      assistant's details, because this page has no use for them. %>
  <section id="ai-agents" class="scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">AI agents</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      An assistant you have connected can read your training and write it: plan a block, log a
      session, correct a set you typed wrong. It sees your account and nothing else. Connecting
      one takes a few steps in the assistant itself, so it has a page of its own.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <a href="/connections" class="font-semibold text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Assistants</a>
      &mdash;
      <%- if @assistants.positive? %>
        <%= @assistants %> assistant<%= 's' unless @assistants == 1 %>
        <%= @assistants == 1 ? 'is' : 'are' %> connected, and can be disconnected there.
      <%- else %>
        nothing is connected yet.
      <%- end %>
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# Where you are, which is the whole of what decides what day it is here. #349.

      This app ran on the server's clock, and the server is UTC, so a lifter in New York at
      half past eight on a Monday evening was on Tuesday as far as it was concerned: signing
      in missed the session they came back to finish, asking an assistant to log "today"
      opened a second empty workout beside the real one, and the session they were halfway
      through was marked missed on the calendar.

      Under one heading with the week start since #529, because they are one idea: both answer
      "when is it, for me", one to the day and one to the week, and neither is any use to
      somebody who came looking for the other. Two forms still, and two saves, for the reason
      the top of this page gives -- the heading is the grouping, not the submission. %>
  <section id="time-and-week" class="scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Time and week</h2>

    <%# The date under the picker is the point of the whole setting, so it is shown rather than
        described: it is either today or it is not, and a lifter can tell at a glance which.

        A list rather than a text box, because "EST" and "GMT+5" are what people type and
        neither resolves -- and it is a curated list rather than all six hundred IANA names,
        because this is set on a phone. Whatever is already stored stays in the list even when
        it is not one of the offerings, so a zone set by hand is never quietly replaced by the
        nearest one on offer. %>
    <h3 class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Time zone</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      What counts as today, which decides which session you land on when you sign in and which
      day a workout is logged against. Unset means UTC.
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="/settings/zone" class="mt-4">
      <%== csrf_tag('/settings/zone') %>
      <label for="time_zone" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Your time zone</label>
      <select name="time_zone" id="time_zone"
              class="<%= field_style %> mt-2 min-h-11 w-full px-2 text-base">
        <option value="">Unset (UTC)</option>
        <%- Tectonic::Clock.zone_options(@time_zone).each do |zone| %>
          <option value="<%= zone %>" <%= 'selected' if zone == @time_zone %>><%= zone %></option>
        <%- end %>
      </select>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
        Today here is <%= @today.strftime('%A %-d %B %Y') %>.
      </p>
      <button type="submit"
              class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold
                     text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
    </form>

    <h3 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Week</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      Which day the calendar grid begins on. It has always begun on Sunday, and not by
      decision &mdash; that is simply the day <code>Date#wday</code> counts from.
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="/settings/week" class="mt-4">
      <%== csrf_tag('/settings/week') %>
      <fieldset>
        <legend class="sr-only">Week starts on</legend>
        <div class="flex gap-2">
          <%- { 0 => 'Sunday', 1 => 'Monday' }.each do |wday, name| %>
            <%# A radio rather than a select, because two options in a menu is a menu that
                hides one of them behind a tap. Labels wrap the input so the whole 44px of it
                is the target. %>
            <%# An instance variable rather than a local, as views/workouts/show.erb does and
                for the reason its comment gives: erb_lint hands each tag to rubocop as a
                snippet of its own, so a local assigned in one tag and read in the next is a
                useless assignment to every cop that sees it. %>
            <%- @chosen = @week_starts_on == wday %>
            <%# The class list's continuation lines sit a step shallower than the attribute
                they belong to, which is not a slip: this section gained an indent when #529
                wrapped it, and the ternary below is one of the longest lines in the app.
                erb_lint hands it to rubocop with the indentation included, and two more
                columns put it over 120. %>
            <label class="<%= button_style %> flex min-h-11 flex-1 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center
                        border px-4 text-sm font-semibold
                        <%= @chosen ? 'border-sky-800 bg-sky-800 text-white' : 'border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700' %>
                        <%= 'hover:bg-gray-50' unless @chosen %>">
              <input type="radio" name="week_starts_on" value="<%= wday %>" class="sr-only" <%= 'checked' if @chosen %>>
              <%= name %>
            </label>
          <%- end %>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> mt-3 bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
    </form>
  </section>

  <%# How long you have to train. #446.

      The column has lived on `programs` since 032 and no block ever carried one, so the
      warning at generation and the comparison in SessionLength were built and never ran. The
      first reading was that the block editor removed in #411 took the only way to set it, and
      that is not quite right: #458 asks for no *CRUD* interface and names the exception, "one
      number per movement and one editable field". This is that.

      It sits here rather than on a block because it is a fact about a week rather than about a
      programme. It will be the same next block and the one after, and asking again on every
      block written is how it comes to be asked never -- which is exactly what happened.

      A block can still say otherwise and wins where it does, because a peaking week really can
      be longer. What changes is that a block saying nothing inherits an answer instead of
      switching the check off.

      Last of the five, and the only one #529 left where it was: it is one number, set once, and
      the section index above is what stops being last meaning being unfindable. %>
  <section id="session-length" class="scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Session length</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      How long you have for a session. When a week is generated, any day estimated to run past
      this is named along with how far over it is. Leave it blank for no warning.
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="/settings/budget" class="mt-4">
      <%== csrf_tag('/settings/budget') %>
      <label for="minutes" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Time for a session</label>
      <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="number" id="minutes" name="minutes" min="10" max="300" step="5" autocomplete="off"
               value="<%= @time_budget_minutes %>"
               class="<%= field_style %> w-28 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">minutes</span>
      </div>
      <button type="submit"
              class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold
                     text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
    </form>
  </section>
</div>
